NNOOTTEESS
       In normal usage, mmttrraaccee() is called once at the start of execution of a
       program, and mmuunnttrraaccee() is never called.

       The tracing output produced after a call to mmttrraaccee() is textual, but
       not designed to be human readable.  The GNU C library provides a Perl
       script, mmttrraaccee(1), that interprets the trace log and produces human‐
       readable output.  For best results, the traced program should be
       compiled with debugging enabled, so that line‐number information is
       recorded in the executable.

       The tracing performed by mmttrraaccee() incurs a performance penalty (if
       MMAALLLLOOCC__TTRRAACCEE points to a valid, writable pathname).

BBUUGGSS
       The line‐number information produced by mmttrraaccee(1) is not always
       precise: the line number references may refer to the previous or
       following (nonblank) line of the source code.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The shell session below demonstrates the use of the mmttrraaccee() function
       and the mmttrraaccee(1) command in a program that has memory leaks at two
       different locations.  The demonstration uses the following program:

           $ ccaatt tt__mmttrraaccee..cc
           #include <mcheck.h>
           #include <stdio.h>
           #include <stdlib.h>

           int
           main(void)
           {
               mtrace();

               for (unsigned int j = 0; j < 2; j++)
                   malloc(100);            /* Never freed--a memory leak */

               calloc(16, 16);             /* Never freed--a memory leak */
               exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
           }

       When we run the program as follows, we see that mmttrraaccee() diagnosed
       memory leaks at two different locations in the program:

           $ cccc --gg tt__mmttrraaccee..cc --oo tt__mmttrraaccee
           $ eexxppoorrtt MMAALLLLOOCC__TTRRAACCEE==//ttmmpp//tt
           $ ..//tt__mmttrraaccee
           $ mmttrraaccee ..//tt__mmttrraaccee $$MMAALLLLOOCC__TTRRAACCEE
           Memory not freed:
           ‐----------------
              Address     Size     Caller
           0x084c9378     0x64  at /home/cecilia/t_mtrace.c:12
           0x084c93e0     0x64  at /home/cecilia/t_mtrace.c:12
           0x084c9448    0x100  at /home/cecilia/t_mtrace.c:16

       The first two messages about unfreed memory correspond to the two
       mmaalllloocc(3) calls inside the _f_o_r loop.  The final message corresponds to
       the call to ccaalllloocc(3) (which in turn calls mmaalllloocc(3)).

